diagnosis_text:
I investigated end-to-end reachability between PC0 (20.1.1.10) and PC12 (120.1.1.10) and cannot reproduce the outage. Topology-wide checks and targeted tests show the path is healthy now: pc0→pc12 and pc12→pc0 both succeed with 0% loss and ~1 ms RTT. Host network configs are consistent: PC0 has 20.1.1.10/8 with default via 20.1.1.1, and PC12 has 120.1.1.10/8 with default via 120.1.1.1. There’s no evidence of interface errors or misconfiguration on either endpoint. The observed TTL=61 in both directions indicates routing is occurring normally through a small number of hops.

Affected component: none detected (ICMP/IP forwarding across the routed path is functioning).
Mechanism: Since all ICMP echo requests/replies complete without loss and with stable latency, any prior packet drops are no longer present; the network is forwarding traffic as expected.
Localization: End hosts pc0.eth0 and pc12.eth0, and the intervening routers; no specific device shows faults at this time.
Root cause: The reported outage is not reproducible now—no persistent misconfiguration or device failure was found.
Corrective fix: No changes required. Recommend monitoring for recurrence; if it returns, capture timestamps and run ping/traceroute from both ends during the event to localize a transient link/routing issue quickly. We can also enable brief logging on edge routers to catch any intermittent flaps if needed. 
